From b088816aa89e3646526cadfa2ccb0613327627f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dong Hwan Kim <karmaddee@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 11:48:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: go back to _sigtramp when Rosetta reports a fault with
 its context.

Under Rosetta 2, a fault in PE code that coincides with a pending
asynchronous signal can reach segv_handler with the wrong context: the
siginfo is the fault's (si_addr 0 for a null read), but the registers are
those of the other signal's entry into _sigtramp, with %rip on its first
instruction and %rsp at the top of the signal stack. segv_handler took
that for a fault on the signal stack, virtual_setup_exception printed
"nested exception on signal stack" and aborted the thread. The thread
ended silently with exit code 0, and a GetThreadContext on it failed.

A thread that takes access violations while another thread suspends it
and reads its context hits this. In tests/gsbaseprobe.c (winecx-gptk),
4 workers take 2000 handled access violations each while the main thread
suspends them 300 to 400 times; without this change a worker was lost in
11 of 18 runs. Unity's garbage collector suspends threads and reads their
context the same way.

When the reported %rip is _sigtramp and %rsp is on the signal stack,
return to the trampoline: the other handler runs, and the instruction
that faulted runs again and faults with its own context once it returns.
The only fault _sigtramp itself can take at that address is its push of
%rbp, which lands just below %rsp, so a fault there is still handled as
before. _sigtramp is looked up once at startup, and only under Rosetta.

With this, 17 runs lost no worker, and in each run whose count was read
every access violation reached the vectored handler exactly once.
GetThreadContext on a live worker still fails now and then with
ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED (2 calls in 1 of the 17 runs); that is a separate
problem.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
index 1fa933e..64b02f6 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
@@ -92,6 +92,10 @@ static BOOL is_rosetta2;
 
 /* CW Hack 23427 */
 static BOOL sequoia_or_later = FALSE;
+
+/* libsystem's signal trampoline, under Rosetta only, see segv_handler */
+#include <dlfcn.h>
+static void *rosetta_sigtramp;
 #endif
 
 static USHORT cs32_sel;  /* selector for %cs in 32-bit mode */
@@ -2439,6 +2443,23 @@ static void segv_handler( int signal, siginfo_t *siginfo, void *_sigcontext )
     struct xcontext context;
     EXCEPTION_RECORD rec = { .ExceptionAddress = (void *)RIP_sig(sigcontext) };
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+    /* Rosetta can deliver a fault together with a pending asynchronous signal (a suspend, say)
+     * and report it with the context of that signal's entry into _sigtramp, on the signal stack,
+     * instead of the instruction that faulted. Taken at face value that is a nested exception
+     * and the thread is aborted. Go back to the trampoline instead: the other handler runs, and
+     * the instruction faults again once it returns. The one real fault at that address is
+     * _sigtramp pushing %rbp, which lands just below %rsp. */
+    if (rosetta_sigtramp && (void *)RIP_sig(sigcontext) == rosetta_sigtramp &&
+        is_inside_signal_stack( data, (void *)RSP_sig(sigcontext) ) &&
+        ((char *)siginfo->si_addr >= (char *)RSP_sig(sigcontext) ||
+         (char *)siginfo->si_addr < (char *)RSP_sig(sigcontext) - 8))
+    {
+        leave_handler( data, sigcontext );
+        return;
+    }
+#endif
+
     save_context( data, &context, sigcontext );
 
     switch(TRAP_sig(sigcontext))
@@ -3067,6 +3088,7 @@ void signal_init_process( TEB *teb )
         else
             is_rosetta2 = ret;
     }
+    if (is_rosetta2) rosetta_sigtramp = dlsym( RTLD_DEFAULT, "_sigtramp" );
 
     /* CW Hack 23427: __builtin_available presumably isn't signal-safe. */
     if (__builtin_available( macOS 15.0, * ))
-- 
